<h2>The Complete Guide to Hiring a 17 Seater Tempo Traveller in Bangalore</h2>
<p>The 17 seater Tempo Traveller is the workhorse of group travel from Bangalore. It carries a full family reunion, a temple group, a college batch, a department outing or a wedding party in one air-conditioned vehicle, at a per-person cost that few other options can match. This guide explains what the vehicle is, who it suits, how its AC and non-AC rates work, how to plan the day, what each popular route costs, and how to avoid the mistakes that waste money and time on large-group trips. Every rate is the live Yogi Tours &amp; Travels tariff: ₹30 per km with AC and ₹28 per km non-AC, a 300 km daily minimum and ₹700 per day driver Bata, with tolls, parking, permits and state taxes billed at actuals.</p>
<p>If you want the cost first, go to <a href="#tt17-cost">Part 5</a>. If you want to compare it with the luxury option, read about the <a href="/17-seater-urbania-for-rent-in-bangalore">17 seater Urbania</a>. If you are deciding between sizes, the <a href="/force-urbania">fleet guide</a> compares every vehicle from 12 to 50 seats.</p>

<h2>Part 1. What a 17 Seater Tempo Traveller Is</h2>

<h3>The vehicle in plain terms</h3>
<p>The Tempo Traveller is the name Indian travellers use for the Force Traveller, a long, high-roof passenger van built by Force Motors. It has been used for group travel across India for decades, and its popularity comes from one thing: it moves many people at a low cost per head without needing the road space or parking of a bus. The 17 seater configuration carries up to 17 passengers plus the driver. Our vehicle is a Force-built van with push-back seats, individual windows, reading lights, charging points and a dedicated luggage boot with a roof carrier, for about 17 bags.</p>
<p>Unlike the luxury Urbania, the Tempo Traveller uses the traditional Traveller body. It is sturdy, simple and easy to maintain, which is why it is trusted on long highway routes and hill roads. It is not the quietest or the smoothest vehicle you can hire. It is the most economical way to carry a full group of up to 17 on a single booking, and for most trips that is exactly what matters.</p>

<h3>What is inside</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Seats:</strong> push-back seats in rows, so each passenger can lean back for rest. Push-back seats are different from full recliners: they tilt a limited amount, which is comfortable for most journeys.</li>
<li><strong>Windows:</strong> individual windows, so most passengers get a view.</li>
<li><strong>Air conditioning:</strong> available on the AC rate. A non-AC version is available at a lower rate (see below).</li>
<li><strong>Reading lights and charging points:</strong> useful on night starts and long drives.</li>
<li><strong>Luggage:</strong> a dedicated boot plus a roof carrier, sized for about 17 bags.</li>
<li><strong>Driver:</strong> an experienced chauffeur who handles the route, the traffic and the parking.</li>
</ul>

<h3>AC or non-AC: which should you pick?</h3>
<p>This is the first real decision on a 17 seater, because the two rates differ by ₹2 per km. On a two-day, 600 km trip, that is ₹1,200 for the whole vehicle, or about ₹70 per person for 17 people.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Choose AC</strong> for anything in the warm months from March to June, for plains routes such as Hampi and Tirupati, for trips with elderly passengers or children, and for any journey of more than four hours.</li>
<li><strong>Consider non-AC</strong> for short winter trips on hill routes, such as Coorg or Ooty between November and January, when the open windows give fresh air and the weather is cool. Non-AC is also reasonable for a budget trip of a few hours.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our recommendation is simple. The saving from non-AC is small compared with the total trip cost, and the comfort difference on a long drive is large. Unless the weather is truly cool or the budget is very tight, choose AC.</p>

