<h2>Part 2. Who Hires a 17 Seater Tempo Traveller</h2>

<h3>Temple and pilgrimage groups</h3>
<p>Pilgrimage groups are the single largest customer for a 17 seater. A group of 15 to 17 relatives, neighbours or community members can share one vehicle and one cost. Typical routes from Bangalore include Tirupati (about 255 km), Mantralaya, Dharmasthala, Kukke Subramanya, Murudeshwar and the Tamil Nadu temple circuits. These trips involve early starts, long waits at temples and late returns, so the vehicle's reliability and the driver's patience matter more than luxury. See <a href="/services/pilgrimage-tours">pilgrimage tours</a> and the <a href="/rent/17-seater-tempo/bangalore-city-to-tirupati">17 seater Tempo Traveller route to Tirupati</a>.</p>

<h3>Large families and reunions</h3>
<p>Three generations travelling together fill a 17 seater easily. Grandparents, parents, aunts, uncles and children sit in one vehicle and arrive together. The cost per person is low enough that families on a modest budget can still make the trip a reality. See <a href="/services/family-tours">family tours</a>.</p>

<h3>College batches, clubs and associations</h3>
<p>College trips, alumni meets, sports clubs and associations of 15 to 17 people use the 17 seater for day trips and weekend tours. A shared vehicle is cheaper than splitting into cars, and the group stays together. Share the list of travellers and the supervising faculty or organiser before departure. See <a href="/services/educational-tours">educational tours</a>.</p>

<h3>Corporate teams and departmental outings</h3>
<p>A department of 15 or 16 can use a 17 seater for an offsite or a team-building day. The vehicle is economical, and a GST invoice can be issued. Ask for the written quotation before the approval process. See <a href="/services/corporate-travel">corporate travel</a>.</p>

<h3>Wedding guests</h3>
<p>For weddings, 17 seater Tempo Travellers move guests between hotels, the venue and the reception. Because several vehicles are often needed, they are cheaper to run than a fleet of cars. See <a href="/services/wedding-transportation">wedding transportation</a>.</p>

<h3>Airport group transfers</h3>
<p>A group arriving at Kempegowda International Airport with luggage fits comfortably in a 17 seater. Share the flight number and the group size. See <a href="/services/airport-transfer">airport transfer</a>.</p>

<h2>Part 3. Seating, Luggage and Space</h2>

<h3>A seating plan that works</h3>
<p>A vehicle with 17 passengers has natural zones. Plan who sits where before you board, because it removes a lot of friction.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Front rows:</strong> elderly passengers, pregnant travellers and anyone prone to motion sickness. The front has the best view and the least movement.</li>
<li><strong>Middle rows:</strong> families with small children, who need aisle access and a steady ride.</li>
<li><strong>Rear rows:</strong> teenagers and able adults. The rear feels bumps more, particularly on broken roads and speed breakers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Rotate seats at lunch so that nobody has the back row for the whole trip.</p>

<h3>The luggage rule</h3>
<p>The boot and roof carrier together are sized for about 17 bags. This works when each person brings one medium bag. It stops working with large suitcases, boxes of gifts, cooking equipment or musical instruments. Count bags before you book. If you carry more than about 17 bags, tell us so we can confirm what fits.</p>
<p>The roof carrier suits soft bags and covered luggage. Valuables, documents, medicines, cameras and electronics should always travel inside the cabin with you.</p>

<h3>Packing for the cabin</h3>
<ul>
<li>One small day bag per person with a water bottle, a phone charger, medicines and a light layer.</li>
<li>A small bag for the group with wet wipes, tissues and a small first-aid kit.</li>
<li>Snacks that are not messy or strongly scented.</li>
<li>Motion-sickness tablets for anyone who needs them.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Part 4. Planning a Large-Group Trip Step by Step</h2>

<h3>Step 1: Fix the headcount</h3>
<p>Collect a final list by name. If the group might grow beyond 17, book the 21 seater mini bus now. A vehicle that is full is comfortable. A vehicle that is overfull is neither safe nor pleasant.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Choose one coordinator</h3>
<p>One person should talk to the driver and to us. That person holds the passenger list, confirms pickup times, keeps the toll receipts and decides on stops. A group with 17 voices and no coordinator is a group that argues.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Plan the pickups</h3>
<p>If everyone is in one area, a single pickup is simple. If people are spread across the city, choose one central meeting point or plan the order of pickups carefully, because each stop adds time. We pick up from any locality. Area pages include <a href="/17seater-tempotraveller/whitefield">Whitefield</a>, <a href="/17seater-tempotraveller/electronic-city">Electronic City</a>, <a href="/17seater-tempotraveller/koramangala">Koramangala</a>, <a href="/17seater-tempotraveller/indiranagar">Indiranagar</a>, <a href="/17seater-tempotraveller/hebbal">Hebbal</a> and <a href="/17seater-tempotraveller/yelahanka">Yelahanka</a>.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Choose the departure time</h3>
<p>Bangalore traffic changes the first hour of any trip. A 5:00 AM departure clears the city quickly, while 8:30 AM on a weekday means slow movement through the IT corridors. For hill destinations, an early start also protects daylight for the ghat sections. Standard duty runs from 6:00 AM to 10:00 PM, and extra Bata applies after 10:00 PM, so plan the return to finish before then.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Build realistic stops</h3>
<p>A group of 17 needs about 30 to 45 minutes for breakfast, about an hour for lunch and a short stop every two hours. A six-hour drive becomes an eight-hour day. Tell the group the real arrival time so nobody feels cheated.</p>

<h3>Step 6: Agree the money</h3>
<p>Decide how the cost will be split, and collect each share before the trip. Appoint the coordinator to settle tolls and parking at the end. A clear plan for money avoids awkward conversations afterwards.</p>

