<h3>Pilgrimage circuits in detail</h3>
<p><strong>Tirupati.</strong> A pre-dawn start of 3:30 or 4:00 AM gets a group to the foothills by mid-morning. Book darshan through the official Tirumala Tirupati Devasthanams channels well before travel, and carry the original ID that matches each booking. Follow the temple's traditional dress code. Because Tirupati is in Andhra Pradesh, interstate permits and state taxes apply and are listed in your quotation. Plan an overnight stay so the driver rests before the return.</p>
<p><strong>Mantralaya.</strong> The town of the Raghavendra Swamy Brindavana, on the Tungabhadra river, lies in Andhra Pradesh and is a long day's drive. Many groups stay one night. Book accommodation ahead during festival periods.</p>
<p><strong>Dharmasthala, Kukke Subramanya and Udupi.</strong> This coastal Karnataka circuit crosses the Western Ghats. The ghat sections need an experienced driver, and monsoon months bring heavy rain, so check conditions before leaving. See the <a href="/tour-packages/dharmasthala-kukke-pilgrimage">Dharmasthala and Kukke package</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Murudeshwar and Gokarna.</strong> The coast of North Kanara has temples and beaches in one trip. It is a longer drive, so plan at least three days.</p>

<h3>Weekend getaways</h3>
<p>Coorg, Chikmagalur and Wayanad are the classic weekend escapes. A 17 seater lets the whole group share the drive and carry trekking bags in the boot and on the roof carrier. Ask your resort whether the access road suits a large van. See the <a href="/tour-packages/coorg-getaway">Coorg Getaway</a> and the <a href="/tour-packages/chikmagalur-coffee-trails">Chikmagalur Coffee Trails</a>.</p>

<h3>Hill roads</h3>
<p>Ooty is the most demanding route because of forest check-posts near Bandipur and Mudumalai, which close overnight. Plan to cross in daylight. Large vehicles take the gentler Gudalur route. See the <a href="/blog/bangalore-to-ooty-road-trip-guide-hairpin-bends-vehicle">Ooty road trip guide</a> and the <a href="/tour-packages/ooty-coonoor-hill-tour">Ooty and Coonoor Hill Tour</a>.</p>

<h2>Part 7. Comfort and Safety</h2>

<h3>Before you leave</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confirm the registration number, the driver's name and phone number the day before.</li>
<li>Check that everyone has ID, medicines and a charged phone.</li>
<li>Share the itinerary with a family member who is not travelling.</li>
<li>Eat lightly before a ghat road.</li>
</ul>

<h3>During the drive</h3>
<p>Wear seat belts where they are fitted. Keep the aisle clear. Do not stand while the vehicle is moving. Ask for a break every two hours. If the driver advises against a late-night dash or a shortcut, take the advice. Experienced chauffeurs know which roads flood, which stretches are poorly lit and which ghat sections are best crossed in daylight.</p>

<h3>For elderly passengers</h3>
<p>Seat them near the door in the front rows. Plan a stop every two hours without waiting to be asked. Carry glucose, water and medicines in the cabin.</p>

<h3>For children</h3>
<p>Bring wipes, a change of clothes, snacks and a motion-sickness plan. Seat children where an adult can reach them. Never leave a child alone in a parked vehicle.</p>

<h3>Weather</h3>
<p>The Western Ghats and the Nilgiris change quickly. Monsoon rain slows traffic and can disrupt some sections. If the driver recommends delaying a start, treat it as protection.</p>

